{"subjectId":"sql","id":"string-functions","title":"String Functions","group":"Functions & Aggregation","order":20,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Built-in functions clean, slice, combine and format text. Names differ between engines more than anywhere else — learn the ideas, then look up the local spelling.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list\"></i>The essentials</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Task</th><th>SQLite / standard</th><th>MySQL</th><th>SQL Server</th><th>PostgreSQL</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Length</td><td><code>length(s)</code></td><td><code>CHAR_LENGTH(s)</code> (<code>LENGTH</code> = bytes)</td><td><code>LEN(s)</code></td><td><code>length(s)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Upper / lower</td><td><code>upper(s)</code>, <code>lower(s)</code></td><td>same</td><td>same</td><td>same</td></tr><tr><td>Substring</td><td><code>substr(s, start, len)</code></td><td><code>SUBSTRING(s, start, len)</code></td><td><code>SUBSTRING(s, start, len)</code></td><td><code>substring(s from 2 for 3)</code></td></tr><tr><td>First / last n</td><td><code>substr(s, 1, n)</code>, <code>substr(s, -n)</code></td><td><code>LEFT(s, n)</code>, <code>RIGHT(s, n)</code></td><td><code>LEFT</code>, <code>RIGHT</code></td><td><code>left</code>, <code>right</code></td></tr><tr><td>Trim</td><td><code>trim(s)</code>, <code>ltrim</code>, <code>rtrim</code></td><td>same</td><td><code>TRIM</code> (2017+), <code>LTRIM</code>, <code>RTRIM</code></td><td>same</td></tr><tr><td>Find position</td><td><code>instr(s, sub)</code></td><td><code>INSTR(s, sub)</code> / <code>LOCATE</code></td><td><code>CHARINDEX(sub, s)</code></td><td><code>position(sub in s)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Replace</td><td><code>replace(s, from, to)</code></td><td>same</td><td>same</td><td>same</td></tr><tr><td>Concatenate</td><td><code>a || b</code>, <code>concat(a, b)</code></td><td><code>CONCAT(a, b)</code></td><td><code>a + b</code>, <code>CONCAT</code></td><td><code>a || b</code>, <code>concat</code></td></tr><tr><td>Pad</td><td><code>printf('%5s', s)</code></td><td><code>LPAD(s, 5, ' ')</code></td><td><code>RIGHT(SPACE(5) + s, 5)</code></td><td><code>lpad(s, 5)</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>SQL strings are <strong>1-indexed</strong>: the first character is position 1, and <code>instr</code>/<code>CHARINDEX</code> return <code>0</code> when not found.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-cut\"></i>Slicing and searching</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT email,\n       length(email)                              AS len,\n       instr(email, '@')                          AS at_pos,\n       substr(email, 1, instr(email, '@') - 1)    AS username,\n       substr(email, instr(email, '@') + 1)       AS domain,\n       substr(email, -2)                          AS last_two\nFROM employees\nWHERE emp_id IN (1, 8)\nORDER BY emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>email          | len | at_pos | username | domain  | last_two\n---------------+-----+--------+----------+---------+---------\nasha@corp.in   | 12  | 5      | asha     | corp.in | in\nvikram@corp.in | 14  | 7      | vikram   | corp.in | in</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-wash\"></i>Cleaning text</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT '[' || trim('   padded   ') || ']'        AS trimmed,\n       ltrim('000742', '0')                      AS no_zeros,\n       replace('2024/03/15', '/', '-')           AS iso_date,\n       lower('MiXeD CaSe')                       AS lowered;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>trimmed  | no_zeros | iso_date   | lowered\n---------+----------+------------+-----------\n[padded] | 742      | 2024-03-15 | mixed case</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-writing\"></i>Building strings</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT name || ' (' || COALESCE(email, 'n/a') || ')'   AS with_pipe,\n       concat(name, ' - ', dept_id)                    AS with_concat,\n       concat_ws(', ', name, email, dept_id)           AS with_separator,\n       printf('%-6s|%8d', name, salary)                AS formatted\nFROM employees\nWHERE emp_id IN (7, 10)\nORDER BY emp_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>with_pipe  | with_concat | with_separator | formatted\n-----------+-------------+----------------+----------------\nNeha (n/a) | Neha - 2    | Neha, 2        | Neha  |   72000\nDev (n/a)  | Dev -       | Dev            | Dev   |   60000</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>||</code> with a <code>NULL</code> operand gives <code>NULL</code> (see Neha's email handled with <code>COALESCE</code>). <code>concat()</code> treats <code>NULL</code> as an empty string in SQLite, PostgreSQL and SQL Server — but in <strong>MySQL</strong> <code>CONCAT()</code> returns <code>NULL</code> if any argument is <code>NULL</code>. <code>concat_ws</code> skips <code>NULL</code>s everywhere (Dev's missing email and department vanish).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-details\"></i>Aggregating strings</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"sql\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>SQL</figcaption><pre><code>SELECT dept_id,\n       group_concat(name, ', ' ORDER BY name) AS team\nFROM employees\nWHERE dept_id IS NOT NULL\nGROUP BY dept_id\nORDER BY dept_id;</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>dept_id | team\n--------+-------------------------\n1       | Asha, Karan, Meera, Ravi\n2       | Arjun, Neha, Priya\n3       | Sara, Vikram</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Spellings: <code>STRING_AGG(name, ', ' ORDER BY name)</code> (PostgreSQL, SQLite 3.44+), <code>STRING_AGG(name, ', ') WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY name)</code> (SQL Server), <code>GROUP_CONCAT(name ORDER BY name SEPARATOR ', ')</code> (MySQL), <code>LISTAGG(name, ', ') WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY name)</code> (Oracle).</p>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you extract the domain from an email in SQL?</summary><div><p>Find the <code>@</code> and take everything after it: <code>substr(email, instr(email, '@') + 1)</code> (SQLite/MySQL), <code>SUBSTRING(email, CHARINDEX('@', email) + 1, LEN(email))</code> (SQL Server), <code>split_part(email, '@', 2)</code> (PostgreSQL), <code>SUBSTRING_INDEX(email, '@', -1)</code> (MySQL).</p></div></details></section>"}